\begin{abstract}
Let $K$ be a field and let $K(\!(q)\!)$ be the field of formal Laurent series in a uniformizer $q$, with its order (valuation) map $\ord$. Call a series \emph{normalized} when it has a simple pole, $\ord f = -1$, and define the \emph{renormalized product} of a family $f_0,\dots,f_{m-1}$ to be $q^m\prod_{i<m} f_i$. We prove that for every $m \ge 1$ the image of the renormalized product map on normalized families is \emph{exactly} the order-zero locus: the total pole order is the only obstruction. We then determine all fibres. For $m=1$ the factorization is unique; for every $m \ge 2$ and every realizable target the fibre is infinite, over every field, including $\mathbb{F}_2$, where no scalar rescaling exists. The fibre is a torsor under the group of order-zero twists, which is free of rank $m-1$; we call $m-1$ the \emph{rigidity index}. Four extensions sharpen the picture: (i) with an arbitrary integer pole profile $d$ and renormalization $q^k$ the realizable set is exactly $\{g : \ord g = k + \sum_{i<m} d_i\}$, and profiles with equal total have canonically bijective fibres via the monomial gauge transformation $f_i \mapsto f_i q^{d_i'-d_i}$; (ii) realizability and non-uniqueness are the two halves of a split short exact sequence $1 \to \ker\Pi \to (\Oun)^m \to \Oun \to 1$ with $\ker\Pi \cong (\Oun)^{m-1}$ as a group; (iii) the theory holds over any commutative group with a $\Zp$-valued valuation and a uniformizer, in particular over $\mathbb{Q}_p$, and truncation modulo $p^D$ gives the exact fibre count $((p-1)p^{D-1})^{m-1}$ with Euler factor $(1-p^{m-1}T)^{-1}$; (iv) over $\mathbb{R}$ with nonnegative coefficients the twist group collapses to the trivial group, so the abundance of factorizations is purely algebraic, and the only law on $\Nat$ with $p_0=1$ whose generating function has a nonnegative reciprocal is the Dirac mass at $0$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

Fix a field $K$ and work in the field $K(\!(q)\!)$ of formal Laurent series: formal sums $\sum_{n \in \Zp} a_n q^n$ with support bounded below. The \emph{order} of a nonzero series is $\ord f = \min\{n : a_n \neq 0\}$, with $\ord 0 = +\infty$. Order is the fundamental invariant: it is a surjective homomorphism $K(\!(q)\!)^\times \to \Zp$, so $\ord(fg) = \ord f + \ord g$.

\begin{definition}[Normalized series]
A Laurent series $f$ is \emph{normalized} if $\ord f = -1$, i.e.\ $f$ has a simple pole at $q=0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Renormalized product]
For $k,m \in \Nat$ and $f : \Nat \to K(\!(q)\!)$ put $R_{k,m}(f) = q^{k}\prod_{i<m} f_i$. The \emph{critical} renormalization is $k=m$; write $R_m := R_{m,m}$.
\end{definition}

Two questions drive the paper. \textbf{Realizability:} for which $g$ do there exist normalized $f_0,\dots,f_{m-1}$ with $R_m(f)=g$? \textbf{Rigidity:} when such a family exists, how large is the set of all of them?

\subsection{Summary of results}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Realizability is governed by a single conservation law: $\ord R_m(f) = m + \sum_{i<m}(-1) = 0$, and conversely every order-zero series is realized, for every $m \ge 1$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:realiz}).
\item Rigidity obeys a sharp dichotomy: unique for $m=1$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:unique1}), infinite for $m \ge 2$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:nonunique}); the fibre is a torsor (Theorem~\ref{thm:torsor}) free of rank $m-1$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:rigidityindex}).
\item The pole profile is pure gauge (Theorems~\ref{thm:profile} and \ref{thm:gauge}).
\item One split short exact sequence explains everything (Section~\ref{sec:exact}).
\item Finite levels count exactly, with an Euler factor whose exponent is the rigidity index (Section~\ref{sec:finite}).
\item Positivity destroys the gauge group (Section~\ref{sec:pos}).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Why this is the natural formulation}

The renormalization is forced: without $q^m$ the realizable set would be the order-$(-m)$ locus, which moves with $m$; the critical choice $k=m$ is the unique one making the realizable set independent of $m$, so that the same target may be compared across slot counts. The obstruction is a conservation law, and the accompanying redundancy --- which slot carries which pole order --- is exactly a gauge freedom. Finally, positivity is a genuine constraint rather than a normalization: probability generating functions live in a proper sub-semiring, and passing to it collapses an infinite gauge group to the trivial one.

\section{Preliminaries}

\begin{lemma}[Order arithmetic]\label{lem:ordarith}
For nonzero $a,b \in K(\!(q)\!)$: $\ord(ab) = \ord a + \ord b$, $\ord(a^{-1}) = -\ord a$, and $\ord(q^k) = k$ for $k \in \Zp$. Moreover $\ord(a+b) \ge \min(\ord a, \ord b)$, with equality when $\ord a \neq \ord b$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{definition}
$\Oun := \{u \in K(\!(q)\!) : \ord u = 0\}$, the group of units of the valuation ring.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Stability of normalization]\label{lem:stab}
If $u \in \Oun$ and $f$ is normalized, then $uf$ is normalized.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\ord(uf) = 0 + (-1) = -1$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:stab} is the engine of every non-uniqueness statement: the normalization condition is invariant under $\Oun$, so units move freely between slots.

\section{Realizability}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:add}
If $f_i$ is normalized for all $i<m$ then $\ord \prod_{i<m} f_i = -m$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:ordR}
For a normalized family, $\ord R_{k,m}(f) = k-m$; in particular $\ord R_m(f)=0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{definition}[Canonical family]
For $g$ of order $0$ and $m \ge 1$ set $\can_m(g)_0 = q^{-1}g$, $\can_m(g)_i = q^{-1}$ for $1 \le i < m$, and $\can_m(g)_i = 1$ for $i \ge m$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Realizability]\label{thm:realiz}
Let $m \ge 1$ and $g \in K(\!(q)\!)$. There exists a family $f$ with $f_i$ normalized for $i<m$ and $R_m(f)=g$ if and only if $\ord g = 0$. Equivalently, $\{R_m(f) : f \text{ normalized}\} = \Oun$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) Proposition~\ref{prop:ordR}. ($\Leftarrow$) Take $f = \can_m(g)$: slot $0$ has order $-1+0=-1$, the other slots order $-1$, and $\prod_{i<m} f_i = q^{-m}g$, so $R_m(f) = q^m q^{-m} g = g$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Arbitrary renormalization exponent]
For $m \ge 1$ and $k \in \Nat$, $\{R_{k,m}(f) : f \text{ normalized on } [0,m)\} = \{g : \ord g = k-m\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Multiplication by $q^{k-m}$ is a bijection from the order-zero locus onto the order-$(k-m)$ locus intertwining the two renormalizations; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:realiz}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:realiz} holds over every field, with no hypothesis on characteristic or cardinality, and no convergence considerations.
\end{remark}

\section{Rigidity: a sharp dichotomy at $m=2$}

\begin{definition}[Factorization set]
For $m \ge 1$ and $\ord g = 0$,
\[
\mathcal{F}_m(g) = \{ f : f_i \text{ normalized for } i<m,\ f_i=1 \text{ for } i \ge m,\ R_m(f)=g\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Uniqueness at $m=1$]\label{prop:unique1}
If $R_1(f) = R_1(f') = g$ then $f_0 = f_0'$, since $q f_0 = q f_0'$ and $q \neq 0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{definition}[Two-slot twist]
For $u \in \Oun$, let $(\tau_u f)_0 = u f_0$, $(\tau_u f)_1 = u^{-1} f_1$, and $(\tau_u f)_i = f_i$ for $i \ge 2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:twistinv}
For $m \ge 2$ and $u \in \Oun$, $\tau_u f$ is normalized on the window whenever $f$ is, and $\prod_{i<m}(\tau_u f)_i = \prod_{i<m} f_i$; hence $R_m(\tau_u f) = R_m(f)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Distinguished units]\label{lem:units}
For $n \in \Nat$ put $u^{(n)} = 1 + q^{n+1}$. Then $\ord u^{(n)} = 0$, $u^{(n)} \neq 0$, $\ord\bigl((u^{(n)})^{-1}\bigr) = 0$, and $n \mapsto u^{(n)}$ is injective.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Non-uniqueness]\label{thm:nonunique}
For every $m \ge 2$ and every $g$ with $\ord g = 0$, the set $\mathcal{F}_m(g)$ is infinite.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The families $\tau_{u^{(n)}}(\can_m(g))$ all lie in $\mathcal{F}_m(g)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:twistinv}, and are pairwise distinct because slot $0$ equals $u^{(n)} q^{-1} g$ with $q^{-1}g \neq 0$, while $n \mapsto u^{(n)}$ is injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Over $\mathbb{F}_2$ there are no nontrivial scalars, so rescaling gives nothing; the units $1+q^{n+1}$ still do. The dichotomy is not an artifact of large coefficient fields.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Fibre $=$ torsor]\label{thm:torsor}
Let $f,f' \in \mathcal{F}_m(g)$. Then $f'_i/f_i \in \Oun$ for $i<m$ and $\prod_{i<m} f'_i/f_i = 1$. Conversely, if $f \in \mathcal{F}_m(g)$ and $u_0,\dots,u_{m-1} \in \Oun$ satisfy $\prod_{i<m} u_i = 1$, then $i \mapsto u_i f_i$ (extended by $1$) lies in $\mathcal{F}_m(g)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Forward: $\ord(f_i'/f_i) = (-1)-(-1) = 0$, and cancelling $q^m \ne 0$ in $R_m(f)=R_m(f')$ gives $\prod f_i = \prod f'_i$. Backward: Lemma~\ref{lem:stab} and $\prod u_i f_i = (\prod u_i)(\prod f_i)$.
\end{proof}

Thus, after choosing a base point, $\mathcal{F}_m(g)$ is identified with the twist group $T_m = \{u \in (\Oun)^m : \prod_{i<m} u_i = 1\}$, trivial exactly when $m=1$ or $\Oun = \{1\}$.

\section{Arbitrary pole profiles}

\begin{definition}[Pole profile]
A family $f$ \emph{has profile} $d : \Nat \to \Zp$ on $[0,m)$ if $\ord f_i = d_i$ for $i<m$ and $f_i=1$ for $i \ge m$. Write $R^{\Zp}_{k,m}(f) = q^k\prod_{i<m} f_i$ for $k \in \Zp$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Profile realizability]\label{thm:profile}
For $m \ge 1$, $k \in \Zp$ and any profile $d$,
\[
\bigl\{R^{\Zp}_{k,m}(f) : f \text{ has profile } d\bigr\} = \Bigl\{g : \ord g = k + \textstyle\sum_{i<m} d_i\Bigr\}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\subseteq$) Orders add. ($\supseteq$) Take $f_i = q^{d_i}$ for $1 \le i<m$ and $f_0 = g\,q^{-k-\sum_{1\le i<m} d_i}$, of order $\ord g - k - \sum_{1\le i<m} d_i = d_0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The classical statement is the constant profile $d \equiv -1$ with $k=m$, recovering Theorem~\ref{thm:realiz}.
\end{corollary}

\section{The abstract valuation setting}

\begin{definition}[Discrete valuation datum]
Let $G$ be a commutative group. A \emph{discrete valuation datum} on $G$ is a pair $(\val,\pi)$ where $\val : G \to \Zp$ satisfies $\val(ab) = \val a + \val b$ and $\pi \in G$ has $\val \pi = 1$. Then $\val 1 = 0$, $\val(a/b) = \val a - \val b$, $\val(a^n) = n\val a$ for $n \in \Zp$, and $\val$ is surjective.
\end{definition}

Write $\Oun_V = \{u \in G : \val u = 0\}$, $R_{k,m}(f) = \pi^k \prod_{i<m} f_i$, and let $\mathcal{F}_{k,m,d}(g)$ be the set of profile-$d$ families with $R_{k,m}(f) = g$.

\begin{theorem}[Abstract realizability]\label{thm:abstract}
For $m \ge 1$, $k \in \Zp$, profile $d$ and $g \in G$: $\mathcal{F}_{k,m,d}(g) \neq \emptyset$ if and only if $\val g = k + \sum_{i<m} d_i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Abstract torsor structure]
Fix $f^0 \in \mathcal{F}_{k,m,d}(g)$. Then $f \mapsto (f_i/f^0_i)_{i<m}$ is a bijection onto $T_m = \{u \in (\Oun_V)^m : \prod u_i = 1\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Rigidity index]\label{thm:rigidityindex}
With $m = n+1$ slots, $T_m$ is in bijection with $(\Oun_V)^n$: slots $1,\dots,n$ are free, slot $0$ is the inverse of their product. Hence for realizable $g$,
\[
\bigl|\mathcal{F}_{k,n+1,d}(g)\bigr| = \bigl|\Oun_V\bigr|^{\,n},
\]
a singleton if and only if $n=0$ or $\Oun_V$ is trivial.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Rigidity dichotomy]
If $\Oun_V \neq \{1\}$ and $g$ is realizable, the factorization is unique if and only if $m=1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{example}[Laurent series]
$G = K(\!(q)\!)^\times$, $\val = \ord$, $\pi = q$ recovers the previous sections.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[$p$-adic numbers]
$G = \mathbb{Q}_p^\times$, $\val = v_p$, $\pi = p$: a $p$-adic number equals $p^k$ times a product of $m$ numbers of prescribed valuations $d_i$ iff $v_p(g) = k + \sum d_i$; for $m \ge 2$ the factorization is never unique, since $\mathbb{Z}_p^\times$ is infinite.
\end{example}

\section{Gauge invariance of the profile}

\begin{definition}[Monomial gauge transformation]
For $e : \Nat \to \Zp$ set $(\gamma_e f)_i = f_i \pi^{e_i}$ for $i<m$ and $(\gamma_e f)_i = f_i$ for $i \ge m$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$\gamma_e$ maps profile-$d$ families to profile-$(d+e)$ families and $\gamma_{-e}\circ\gamma_e = \mathrm{id}$. If $\sum_{i<m} e_i = 0$ then $R_{k,m}(\gamma_e f) = R_{k,m}(f)$, since $\prod_{i<m}\pi^{e_i} = \pi^{\sum e_i} = 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Profiles with equal total are gauge-equivalent]\label{thm:gauge}
Let $\sum_{i<m} d_i = \sum_{i<m} d'_i$. Then for every $k$ and $g$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the realizable sets coincide;
\item $\gamma_{d'-d}$ is a bijection $\mathcal{F}_{k,m,d}(g) \to \mathcal{F}_{k,m,d'}(g)$;
\item $\mathcal{F}_{k,m,d}(g)$ is a singleton (or empty) iff $\mathcal{F}_{k,m,d'}(g)$ is.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

The physical reading: the $d_i$ are gauge coordinates, the total $\sum d_i$ is the gauge-invariant observable, and the transformations $\gamma_e$ with $\sum e_i = 0$ form the gauge group. No feature of the problem can distinguish profiles with equal total.

\section{The split exact sequence behind the dichotomy}\label{sec:exact}

Let $\Pi : (\Oun)^m \to \Oun$, $\Pi(u) = \prod_{i<m} u_i$, be the product homomorphism.

\begin{theorem}[Splitting]
For $m \ge 1$, the map $\sigma : \Oun \to (\Oun)^m$ placing $u$ in slot $0$ and $1$ elsewhere is a homomorphism with $\Pi \circ \sigma = \mathrm{id}$. In particular $\Pi$ is surjective.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Kernel is free of rank $m-1$]\label{thm:kernel}
For $m = n+1$ there is a group isomorphism $\ker\Pi \cong (\Oun)^n$, $u \mapsto (u_1,\dots,u_n)$, with inverse $(w_1,\dots,w_n) \mapsto \bigl((w_1\cdots w_n)^{-1},w_1,\dots,w_n\bigr)$. Consequently $(\Oun)^m \cong \ker\Pi \times \Oun$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Fibre $=$ kernel]
For realizable $g$ and any profile $d$, $\mathcal{F}_{k,m,d}(g)$ is in bijection with $\ker\Pi$; it is a singleton iff $\ker\Pi$ is trivial, iff $m=1$ or $\Oun = \{1\}$.
\end{theorem}

Thus the sequence
\[
1 \longrightarrow \ker\Pi \longrightarrow (\Oun)^m \xrightarrow{\ \Pi\ } \Oun \longrightarrow 1
\]
is exact and split: surjectivity of $\Pi$ is realizability, nontriviality of $\ker\Pi$ is non-uniqueness, and the rank of $\ker\Pi$ is the rigidity index $m-1$, the corank of the product map.

\section{Finite levels, exact counts, and an Euler factor}\label{sec:finite}

For a commutative group $U$ put $\Phi_n(U,g) = \{f : \{0,\dots,n\} \to U \mid \prod_{i=0}^n f_i = g\}$.

\begin{theorem}[Free slots]\label{thm:freeslots}
For every commutative group $U$, every $n \ge 0$ and every $g \in U$ there is a bijection $\Phi_n(U,g) \cong U^n$, $f \mapsto (f_1,\dots,f_n)$, with inverse $(w_1,\dots,w_n)\mapsto\bigl(g(w_1\cdots w_n)^{-1},w_1,\dots,w_n\bigr)$. Hence $|\Phi_n(U,g)| = |U|^n$ for finite $U$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-level dichotomy]
For finite $U$: $|\Phi_n(U,g)| = 1$ iff $n=0$ or $|U|=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-level fibre count]\label{thm:count}
Let $p$ be prime, $D \ge 1$, $m = n+1$. For every $g \in (\Zp/p^D)^\times$,
\[
\bigl|\Phi_n\bigl((\Zp/p^D)^\times, g\bigr)\bigr| = \bigl((p-1)p^{D-1}\bigr)^n,
\]
since $|(\Zp/p^D)^\times| = \varphi(p^D) = (p-1)p^{D-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Level recursion]
For $D \ge 1$, the level-$(D+1)$ count equals $p^n$ times the level-$D$ count: each extra digit of precision multiplies the number of factorizations by $p^n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Euler factor of the rigidity index]\label{thm:euler}
For every $N \in \Nat$ and every $T$ in a commutative ring,
\[
\bigl(1-p^n T\bigr)\sum_{D=0}^{N-1}\bigl((p-1)p^{D}\bigr)^{n} T^{D+1} = (p-1)^n\,T\,\bigl(1-(p^nT)^N\bigr).
\]
Formally, $\sum_{D\ge 1} |\Phi_n|_D\, T^D = \dfrac{(p-1)^nT}{1-p^nT}$: the denominator is exactly $1-p^{m-1}T$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Factor $((p-1)p^D)^n = (p-1)^n (p^n)^D$ out of the sum and apply $(1-x)\sum_{D<N}x^D = 1-x^N$ with $x = p^nT$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-level rigidity dichotomy]
If $(p-1)p^{D-1} \ge 2$ --- i.e.\ $p$ odd, or $p=2$ and $D \ge 2$ --- then $|\Phi_n| = 1$ iff $n = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The exceptional level]
For $p=2$, $D=1$ the group $(\Zp/2)^\times$ is trivial, so $|\Phi_n| = 1$ for every $n$: modulo $2$ the factorization is unique regardless of the number of slots. This is the unique case where finite-level rigidity is strictly stronger than valuation-level rigidity.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Lifting]
If $\varphi : U \to V$ is a surjective homomorphism of commutative groups, the induced map $\Phi_n(U,g)\to\Phi_n(V,\varphi(g))$, $f \mapsto \varphi\circ f$, is surjective. Applied to $(\Zp/p^{D+1})^\times \twoheadrightarrow (\Zp/p^D)^\times$: every factorization modulo $p^D$ lifts, so the tower of fibres has surjective transition maps.
\end{theorem}

\section{Probability: the bridge and the collapse of the gauge group}\label{sec:pos}

\subsection{The bridge}

\begin{definition}
For a weight sequence $c : \Nat \to K$ and cutoff $N$, put $G_c^N = \sum_{n<N} c_n q^n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:gforder}
If $N \ge 1$ and $c_0 \neq 0$ then $\ord G_c^N = 0$. If $c_0 = 0$ then $\ord G_c^N > 0$ (possibly $+\infty$).
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $G_c^N = c_0 + q\bigl(\sum_{n<N-1}c_{n+1}q^n\bigr)$; the shifted part has order $\ge 1$, and Lemma~\ref{lem:ordarith} gives equality of orders when $c_0 \ne 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Probability bridge]
Let $c$ be a finitely supported weight sequence with cutoff $N \ge 1$ and let $m \ge 1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $c_0 \neq 0$ then $G_c^N = q^m\prod_{i<m} f_i$ for some normalized $f_0,\dots,f_{m-1}$.
\item If $c_0 = 0$ then no such factorization exists, for any $m \ge 1$.
\item For $K = \mathbb{R}$, $c=p$ a nonnegative law with $p_0>0$, and $m \ge 2$, the set of such factorizations is infinite.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

In words: a finitely supported law admits a renormalized factorization into simple-pole factors precisely when it charges the atom at $0$.

\subsection{Positivity destroys the twists}

Call a formal power series $u = \sum_n u_n x^n$ over $\mathbb{R}$ \emph{nonnegative} if $u_n \ge 0$ for all $n$.

\begin{theorem}[Convolution obstruction]\label{thm:conv}
Let $u,v$ be nonnegative power series over $\mathbb{R}$ with $uv=1$. Then $u_n = 0$ for all $n \ge 1$; that is, $u$ is a constant, and $u_0 > 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $n \ge 1$ the $n$-th coefficient of $uv=1$ is $\sum_{j=0}^n u_j v_{n-j} = 0$, a sum of nonnegative terms; hence every term vanishes, in particular $u_nv_0 = 0$. From $u_0v_0 = 1$ we get $v_0 \neq 0$, so $u_n = 0$. Positivity of $u_0$ follows from $u_0v_0=1$ with both factors nonnegative.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Trivial positive twist group]
If moreover $u_0=1$ then $u=1$: the two-slot twist that generates non-uniqueness is never positivity-preserving unless trivial.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Dirac rigidity]
Let $p$ be a nonnegative sequence with $p_0=1$ whose generating function has a nonnegative reciprocal. Then $p_n = \mathbb{1}[n=0]$.
\end{corollary}

Over a field the fibre is an infinite torsor with rigidity index $m-1$; imposing nonnegativity collapses the acting group to $\{1\}$. The abundance of algebraic factorizations is invisible to probability.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithmx}[Canonical factorization]
Given $g$ with $\ord g = k + \sum_{i<m} d_i$, set $f_i = q^{d_i}$ for $1 \le i<m$ and $f_0 = g\,q^{-k-\sum_{1\le i<m}d_i}$. Cost $O(m)$; correctness by Theorem~\ref{thm:abstract}.
\end{algorithmx}

\begin{algorithmx}[Twist enumeration]
Given a factorization $f$ and units $u_1,\dots,u_n$ ($n = m-1$) of valuation $0$, output $f_0' = f_0(u_1\cdots u_n)^{-1}$, $f_i' = u_i f_i$. Every fibre element arises exactly once (Theorems~\ref{thm:kernel}, \ref{thm:freeslots}). Cost $O(m)$ multiplications.
\end{algorithmx}

\begin{algorithmx}[Gauge transport]
Given a profile-$d$ factorization and $d'$ with $\sum d = \sum d'$, output $f_i \mapsto f_i q^{d'_i-d_i}$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:gauge}). Cost $O(m)$ shifts.
\end{algorithmx}

\begin{algorithmx}[Finite-level enumeration]
Given $p,D,n$ and $g \in (\Zp/p^D)^\times$, iterate over $(w_1,\dots,w_n)$ and prepend $g(w_1\cdots w_n)^{-1}$, producing all $((p-1)p^{D-1})^n$ tuples. Cost $\Theta(n\varphi(p^D)^n)$; the count alone is $O(1)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:count}.
\end{algorithmx}

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{What is being classified.} Once the profile is absorbed by gauge, the renormalized product map \emph{is} the product homomorphism $\Pi$ on $(\Oun)^m$: it is surjective (realizability), split (canonical factorization), with kernel free of rank $m-1$ (rigidity). The richness of the original question is the richness of the group $\Oun$, transported.

\paragraph{Why the total valuation is the only obstruction.} Because $\val$ is surjective: the uniformizer allows an arbitrary deficit to be dumped into one slot. With values in a proper subgroup such as $2\Zp$ a second, arithmetic obstruction would appear.

\paragraph{The role of positivity.} The group $\Oun$ owes its size to additive inverses: $1+q$ has inverse $1-q+q^2-\cdots$, which is nonnegative only for constants. The units of the semiring of nonnegative series are exactly the positive constants, whereas the units of the ring of all series are all series with nonzero constant term. The rigidity index is a property of the ambient unit group, and semirings have far fewer units than rings.

\paragraph{Finite levels as a bridge.} The invariant $m-1$ survives truncation as the exponent in the Euler factor $(1-p^{m-1}T)^{-1}$; surjective transition maps make the finite levels determine the limit, except at $p=2$, $D=1$.

\paragraph{Independence of the coefficient field.} Non-uniqueness holds over $\mathbb{F}_2$: the twists $1+q^{n+1}$ are built from the uniformizer alone, with no scalars involved.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Full probabilistic rigidity.} Characterize, for a fixed law with $p_0>0$ and fixed $m$, the set of factorizations whose factors are themselves rescaled probability generating functions; the natural conjecture is that this fibre is always a singleton, i.e.\ the positive rigidity index is $0$.
\item \textbf{Non-surjective valuations and higher rank.} Replace $\Zp$ by an arbitrary ordered value group or drop the uniformizer, and quantify the resulting arithmetic obstruction.
\item \textbf{Rigidity indices elsewhere.} Identify the analogue of $m-1$ for decomposition problems with prescribed residues, symmetry, or integrality constraints.
\item \textbf{Euler products.} Assemble the local factors $(1-p^{m-1}T)^{-1}$ over all primes with $T = p^{-s}$ into a zeta-like object and study its analytic behaviour.
\item \textbf{Effective enumeration.} Uniform sampling from a finite-level fibre, and enumeration under extra congruence constraints.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

For every $m \ge 1$ the realizable targets are exactly the series of order $0$ --- in the general valued setting, exactly those of valuation $k+\sum_{i<m}d_i$. The pole profile is pure gauge. The fibre over a realizable target is a torsor under a group free of rank $m-1$, so factorization is unique precisely for one slot and infinitely ambiguous for two or more, over every field. All of this is the split short exact sequence $1 \to \ker\Pi \to (\Oun)^m \to \Oun \to 1$ in disguise. Truncating to finite precision turns the rigidity index into the exponent of an Euler factor; and demanding positivity annihilates the gauge group, so that unbounded algebraic ambiguity becomes, for probability distributions, a single canonical decomposition.

